<% docs_base = "https://github.com/NahuelRibera/DataCheck/blob/main" %>
<div class="landing">
  <section class="landing__hero" aria-labelledby="landing-title">
    <h1 id="landing-title">Check HR data before it reaches your database</h1>
    <p class="landing__lead">
      DataCheck is a workbench for migrating legacy employee CSV files. It profiles the file,
      reports problems, shows what a migration would change, runs it only when nothing blocks it,
      and then checks the result against the database. Every step is recorded in an audit trail.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <%= link_to "Open the dashboard", dashboard_path, class: "btn btn--primary" %>
      <%= link_to "Start a new import", new_import_run_path, class: "btn" %>
      <%= link_to "Read the documentation", "#{docs_base}/docs/architecture.md", class: "btn" %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="landing__section" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
    <h2 id="problem-title">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Legacy exports tend to contain duplicate employee IDs, malformed dates, unknown departments
      and broken manager references. Loading such a file straight into a live system makes the
      damage hard to find and harder to undo. DataCheck is for people who own that migration and
      need to know what is wrong with the file, and what a load will do, before anything is written.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="landing__section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <h2 id="workflow-title">How an import works</h2>
    <ol class="landing__steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Upload and preview</h3>
        <p>Add a CSV. The preview shows inferred column types, empty and duplicate counts, and the first rows, with personal columns masked.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Preflight</h3>
        <p>Every row is checked. Problems are recorded as blocking issues or warnings, referenced by row number, field and employee ID.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Dry run</h3>
        <p>Shows how many employees would be created, updated or skipped, using the same classification as the real run, without writing any employee records.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Execute</h3>
        <p>Runs the migration in a single transaction. It is refused while the run still has blocking issues.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Verify</h3>
        <p>Integrity checks are recomputed from the database after the fact, not taken from the counters the import wrote.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Audit</h3>
        <p>Each step is appended to an ordered audit trail you can read on the import run page.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="landing__section" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
    <h2 id="safety-title">Safety properties that are implemented</h2>
    <ul class="landing__list">
      <li><strong>Dry run never writes employees.</strong> It shares the migration plan code with execute but does not persist.</li>
      <li><strong>Blocked runs cannot execute.</strong> The check lives in the execute service, not only in the web interface.</li>
      <li><strong>Imports are idempotent.</strong> Re-running the same clean file creates nothing new; a database uniqueness constraint on company and employee ID backs this up.</li>
      <li><strong>No raw personal values in records.</strong> Issues and audit events refer to row numbers, field names and employee IDs rather than names, emails or salaries. A test with canary values covers the pipeline.</li>
      <li><strong>Read-only analysis.</strong> The Python profiler only reads the CSV; Rails alone writes to the database.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="landing__note">
      All data in this project is synthetic. DataCheck is a portfolio project and does not currently include user authentication.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="landing__section" aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <h2 id="next-title">Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="landing__list">
      <li><%= link_to "Dashboard", dashboard_path %> with recent import runs and their status</li>
      <li><%= link_to "All import runs", import_runs_path %></li>
      <li><%= link_to "Architecture documentation", "#{docs_base}/docs/architecture.md" %></li>
      <li><%= link_to "Incident case study", "#{docs_base}/docs/incident-case-study.md" %></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
